module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.ComputingIntersectionMultiplicities.HilbertPolynomialMultiplicity

/-!
# The intersection multiplicity with an effective Cartier divisor as a length

Let `X` be a nonsingular variety, let `a` be a closed point of the projective line and let
`W ⊆ X × P¹` be a closed subvariety of dimension `k + 1` which intersects the fibre `X × a`
properly. The fibre `X × a` is an effective Cartier divisor in `X × P¹`, and the scheme-theoretic
fibre `W_a` of `W → P¹` over `a` is the scheme-theoretic intersection of `W` with `X × a`. Passing
to the local ring `A = 𝒪_{X × P¹, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of an irreducible component `Z` of
`W ∩ X × a`, which is a regular local ring, the data becomes two ideals of `A`: the ideal
`I = (f₁)` of the divisor, generated by a single nonzerodivisor, and the ideal `J` of `W`, whose
quotient `A ⧸ J = 𝒪_{W, ξ}` is a domain because `W` is a subvariety. That `Z` is an irreducible
component of the intersection says `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0`, and that `Z` has dimension `k` while
`W` has dimension `k + 1` says `dim (A ⧸ J) = 1`.

Under these hypotheses the Tor-formula intersection multiplicity of the pair is the length of
`A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)`, the local ring of the scheme-theoretic intersection:

`e(X × P¹, W · X × a, Z) = length_{𝒪_{X × P¹, ξ}} 𝒪_{W_a, ξ}`.

The right-hand side is the multiplicity of `Z` in the closed subscheme `W_a`, that is, the
coefficient of `Z` in the associated cycle `[W_a]_k`. So this is the coefficient identity that
underlies `[W_a]_k = W · X × a`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Intersection.length_quotient_sup_eq_length_quotient_smul_top`: cutting `A ⧸ J` by the
  ideal `I` produces `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)`, so the two lengths agree.
* `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_quotient_sup_of_isDomain`: the
  Tor-formula multiplicity of the pair is the length of the local ring of the scheme-theoretic
  intersection.

## Implementation notes

Proper intersection is not taken as a hypothesis. For an ideal generated by a regular sequence of
length one, `dim (A ⧸ I) + 1 = dim A`, so `dim (A ⧸ J) = 1` already gives
`dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`, with equality. Dimensions are `ringKrullDim`, valued in
`WithBot ℕ∞`, so the comparison with the source's `dim Z = k` carries no subtraction.

### What this file carries of Tag 0B07

Tag 0B07 is registered on `Stacks.Chow.tag_0B07`, which states the whole of it at an arbitrary
pair of distinct closed points of `ℙ¹`. What this file carries of the tag is part (1)(a), and
only in the local form at the generic
point of a component of the intersection, which is the form the source's proof establishes:
the identity of the coefficient of a single component, with the effective Cartier divisor
replaced by an ideal of the local ring generated by one nonzerodivisor and the subvariety by an
ideal with domain quotient.

The rest of the tag is carried elsewhere, on the Section 43.14 product of Tag 0AZR
(`AlgebraicGeometry.TorFormula.intersectionProductCycle`) and its bilinear extension of Tag
0B08:

* the cycle identity of part (1)(a) is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.TorFormula.subschemeCycle_sup_eq_intersectionProductCycle_of_mem_support`;
* part (1)(b), `[W_a]_k = pr_{X, *}(W · X × a)` as cycles on `X`, is
  `Stacks.Chow.properPushforward_intersectionProductCycle_eq_zeroFiberCycle` and its infinity
  twin;
* part (2), that `pr_{X, *}(α · X × a - α · X × b)` is rationally equivalent to zero, is
  `Stacks.Chow.sub_properPushforward_torFormulaCycleIntersectionProduct_mem_rationalEquivalences`;
* part (3), the converse, is
  `Stacks.Chow.isFibreIntersectionDifference_of_mem_rationalEquivalences`.

Those four are stated over an arbitrary Chow situation and therefore carry regularity of the
ambient `X × ℙ¹` as a hypothesis. For a nonsingular variety over an algebraically closed field —
the source's own setting — it is derived instead, in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.TorFormula.FibreCycleNonsingularVariety`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B07](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B07)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B05](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B05)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AZR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AZR)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

universe u

namespace Stacks.Intersection

/-- The local ring of the scheme-theoretic intersection has the same length as the quotient of
`A ⧸ J` by the ideal `I`. -/
theorem length_quotient_sup_eq_length_quotient_smul_top {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    (I J : Ideal A) :
    Module.length A (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) =
      Module.length A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ (I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)))) :=
  ((quotSmulTopEquivQuotSup I J).length_eq).symm

/-- A one-element regular sequence drops the dimension by one, so a quotient `A ⧸ J` of dimension
one already makes the pair intersect properly. -/
private lemma ringKrullDim_le_add_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_one {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A] (f : Fin 1 → A) (I J : Ideal A)
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (hJ : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) = 1) :
    ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) := by
  have h := ringKrullDim_add_length_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isRegular (List.ofFn f) hf
  rw [Ideal.ofList_ofFn, ← hI, List.length_ofFn] at h
  rw [hJ, ← h]
  simp

/-- **Stacks 0B07, part (1)(a) in local form at the generic point of a component.** Let `A` be a
regular local ring, let `I = (f₁)` be generated by a one-element regular sequence, and let `J` be
an ideal with `A ⧸ J` a domain, `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` and `dim (A ⧸ J) = 1`. Then the
Tor-formula intersection multiplicity of the pair is the length of `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)`.

Geometrically, for `A = 𝒪_{X × P¹, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of an irreducible component `Z`
of `W ∩ X × a`, with `I` the local ideal of the effective Cartier divisor `X × a` and `J` the
local ideal of the closed subvariety `W` of dimension `k + 1`, the conclusion is
`e(X × P¹, W · X × a, Z) = length_{𝒪_{X × P¹, ξ}} 𝒪_{W_a, ξ}`, because the scheme-theoretic
fibre `W_a` is the scheme-theoretic intersection of `W` with `X × a`. The right-hand side is the
coefficient of `Z` in `[W_a]_k`, so this is the coefficient identity underlying
`[W_a]_k = W · X × a`. -/
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_quotient_sup_of_isDomain {A : Type u}
    [CommRing A] [IsRegularLocalRing A] (f : Fin 1 → A) (I J : Ideal A) [IsDomain (A ⧸ J)]
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hJ : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) = 1) :
    I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) =
      ((Module.length A (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J))).toNat : ℚ) := by
  rw [length_quotient_sup_eq_length_quotient_smul_top]
  exact localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_quotient_smul_of_isDomain f I J hf hI h0
    (ringKrullDim_le_add_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_one f I J hf hI hJ)

end Stacks.Intersection

end
